  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 PM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nThis Weekend... High pressure overhead today allowed for clear \nskies this morning into the early afternoon. The heating of \natmosphere then allowed for two thing to occur: diurnal fair \nweather cumulus clouds and mixing. Winds have varied across the \nbroader region due to the winds aloft available to mix down. We\nhave good mixing everywhere, but it is more from central \nMinnesota to western Wisconsin that has higher winds aloft and \ntherefore higher winds brought down to the surface. As we start \nto cool this evening along the usual diurnal curve the skies \nwill clear and the winds will become light. With clear skies and\nlight winds we will see ample radiational cooling tonight. \nThis will allow temperatures to fall into the mid 40s to mid \n50s. For these cooler areas, east central MN and northwest WI, \nsome fog formation is possible. Sunday during the day will be a\nsimilar setup to Saturday with the high pressure system starting\nto move out, but still present. Another day warming up into the\nupper 70s, but there are not as high of winds aloft as the \nupper air pattern shifts and it will therefore not be as gusty \noutside. This shift in the pattern will have a bigger impact on \nSunday night. Temperatures will not cool as much over western \nMinnesota as clouds move in. This will provide for a wider \ntemperature gap with lows near the SD/MN border near 60 and in \nwestern WI temperatures will fall into the 40s. These clouds are\ntied to a larger system over the High Plains. Some of the rain \nshowers associated with this system could impact parts of the \nsouthern Minnesota River Valley Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. Due to the time of day there is little instability, so\nrain showers are most likely rather than thunderstorms.\n\nNext Week... The main driver early in the week on Monday and\nTuesday will be a shortwave moving across the Canadian Prairies.\nThe associated surface low and its fronts will be the main \nsource for lift driving the weather. The biggest question for us\nhere continues to be moisture, as many forecast soundings \ncontinue to show lots of dry air to overcome. The timing of the \nfrontal passage will limit much of the strong to severe storm \nchances. However a weak jet streak across North Dakota into \nnorthwest Minnesota (better shear) combined with a slightly \nearlier timing (more instability) could allow for some stronger\nstorms in parts of west central Minnesota. For this reason SPC \nhas a marginal severe risk out for this area. On the larger \nscale synoptic impacts there are still differences between the \ntiming of the frontal passage and amount of moisture between \nensemble members. For this reason any specific 6 hour period is \nhard to provide much more than a 40 percent chance. When the \nwhole window is taken into account the grand majority of members\nprovide at least some QPF. So bottom line, we will have to get \ninto the higher resolution windows to gain more confidence on \ndetails but confidence on seeing rain across these two days has\ngone up. Past this it looks fairly quiet for the rest of the \nweek with maybe some signal for something at the end of the \nweek, but it is a little early to say much about that.",
